crtsh/ and crtsh.json: the top-up, read from crt.sh's public PostgreSQL database (host crt.sh, port 5432, database
  certwatch, user guest) from 2026-10-29T14:02:24Z to 2026-10-29T23:06:44Z, the end of the last run that queried it, by
  tools/ct-seed/topup.py
  (crtsh.json's versions: cryptography 46.0.3, psycopg 3.3.6, python 3.14.7, zlib 1.2.12):
    uv run --with 'psycopg[binary]==3.3.6' --with 'cryptography==46.0.3' python3 -I tools/ct-seed/topup.py \
        --index-keys ct-seed/index-keys.txt --sample ct-seed/logs --per-issuer 120 --cache <dir> --out ct-seed
  resumed on the same cache after interruptions, some legs with --batch 1, one with --retry-timeouts. The legs ran
  earlier versions of the tool with the same query, crtsh.json's (its SHA-256 was added to the cache afterwards, from
  the tool's text, which no leg changed); the last run, with the version hashed below, wrote every output from the
  cache without querying crt.sh, and crtsh.json's finished is that run's. crtsh-ca.json is the cache's map: the start,
  the cap, the SHA-256s of the index keys and of the query, and each key's crt.sh CA ids and the public key they
  share. The earlier tool versions' hashes and the duplicate rows are not recorded, so crtsh/ can be checked (each
  leaf's signature under its key in crtsh-ca.json) but not rebuilt. For each index key, the crt.sh CAs whose public
  key hashes to it (1036 CAs for 1034 keys; 1 key has none), and for each CA at most 120 of its most recently issued
  certificates (issued in the 400 days before the start) that are valid at the start and cannot issue certificates.
  crt.sh returned 71629 certificates: 0 rejected (structure or signature), 0 filtered (not valid at the start, or a
  CA), 15 duplicates of a TBSCertificate already kept or in logs/, 0 past the cap, and 71614 kept, for 927 keys; for
  58 keys crt.sh returned none. The queries of 49 CAs, one for each of 49 keys, reached crt.sh's statement timeout on
  every try and are recorded in crtsh.json as their keys' errors: CA ids 904, 64135, 158799, 158800, 204407, 204411,
  221388, 247106, 247107, 247108, 247110, 247112, 247114, 247115, 247116, 247118, 247120, 282053, 282054, 286234,
  286236, 286242, 286243, 286244, 291200, 391370, 405762, 412165, 422998, 439355, 439892, 439906, 439910, 439917,
  439918, 439929, 439930, 440026, 440027, 440028, 440029, 440032, 440033, 440034, 440035, 440042, 440043, 440044,
  440061.
  trust: crt.sh's database served TLS under a certificate that had expired on 2026-06-21 (seen on 2026-10-29), so the
  connection is encrypted but not authenticated, and nothing crt.sh sends is trusted. A CA's public key is used only
  when its SHA-256 is the index key, and a certificate is kept only when it is one DER Certificate with nothing after
  it, minimal lengths in the headers checked (the Certificate, the TBSCertificate's header, the AlgorithmIdentifier,
  its OID, the BIT STRING) and a v3 TBSCertificate, whose signature verifies under that key, with no TBSCertificate
  kept twice; an attacker on the path can withhold certificates, which the counts show, but not add one its issuer
  did not sign. revokt's CtSeedTest (revokt spec revision 45) verifies every kept leaf again on the JDK.

